    "q-12568": {
      "dom_id": "step-q12568",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(3x)\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(3x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
